<!-- l4: decide:which limb for -->

**35.** In relation to an InvestorFacts “facts”, which limb for means if facts's an accredited investor, then None. Rule 100(a)(2) bites only where the purchaser is not an accredited investor — a carve-out added on 2021-03-15.; otherwise, if either annual income or net worth is less than the cut point with investor profile from with facts, then 17 CFR 227.100(a)(2)(i); otherwise, 17 CFR 227.100(a)(2)(ii).

<!-- l4: decide:how the limit is worked out for -->

**36.** In relation to an InvestorFacts “facts”, how the limit is worked out for means if facts's an accredited investor, then concatenate You are an accredited investor, so Regulation Crowdfunding sets no limit on what you may invest (17 CFR 227.100(a)(2), as amended with effect from 2021-03-15). The figure shown is what your limit would be if you were not: , dollars with limit for with facts and .; otherwise, if either annual income or net worth is less than the cut point with investor profile from with facts, then concatenate Rule 100(a)(2)(i) governs, because at least one of your annual income (, dollars with facts's your annual income, ) and your net worth (, dollars with facts's your net worth, ) is below , dollars with income or net worth cut point, . Your limit is , limb (i) percentage as string, % of the greater of the two (, dollars with greater of annual income or net worth with investor profile from with facts, ), or , dollars with minimum permitted investment, , whichever is the larger — which comes to , dollars with limit for with facts and .; otherwise, concatenate Rule 100(a)(2)(ii) governs, because both your annual income (, dollars with facts's your annual income, ) and your net worth (, dollars with facts's your net worth, ) are at least , dollars with income or net worth cut point, . Your limit is , limb (ii) percentage as string, % of the greater of the two (, dollars with greater of annual income or net worth with investor profile from with facts, ), capped at , dollars with maximum amount sold to any one investor in a 12-month period,  — which comes to , dollars with limit for with facts and ..

<!-- l4: decide:investment caveats -->

**37.** Investment caveats means list of The limit is measured against the GREATER of your annual income and your net worth. Until 2021-03-15 it was measured against the LESSER, and Release 33-10884 substituted the word. A guide that still says 'lesser' is not carrying a stale number; it is carrying a policy the Commission abandoned, and it will understate what you may lawfully invest whenever your income and your net worth differ., The limit runs across ALL Regulation Crowdfunding offerings you invest in during the 12 months before the transaction, not just this one., Rule 100(a)(2) does not define 'annual income' or 'net worth'. Instruction 1 sends you to the accredited-investor calculation in Rule 501, and Instruction 2 allows spouses to calculate jointly subject to a per-individual cap. Neither is modelled here: the figures are read as your own. and 'Accredited investor' takes its meaning from Rule 501 of Regulation D. This check takes your answer at face value..

## Group 8 — may these securities be transferred yet

<!-- l4: decide:days still to wait for -->

**38.** In relation to a ResaleFacts “holding”, days still to wait for means if transfer falls within an exception in Rule 501(a) with transfer from with holding, then 0; otherwise, never below zero with the difference between holding's date of the transfer and the first anniversary of the issuance with transfer from with holding.

<!-- l4: decide:which part of Rule 501(a)(4) applies to -->

**39.** In relation to a ResaleFacts “holding”, which part of Rule 501(a)(4) applies to means if holding's to a family member or the equivalent, then The transferee is a member of your family or the equivalent, which Rule 501(a)(4) excepts from the restriction. Rule 501(c) defines the term, and defines it with 'includes', so its list of fourteen relationships is not closed.; otherwise, if holding's to a trust you control, then The transferee is a trust you control, which Rule 501(a)(4) excepts from the restriction.; otherwise, if holding's to a trust for your family, then The transferee is a trust created for the benefit of a member of your family or the equivalent, which Rule 501(a)(4) excepts from the restriction.; otherwise, if holding's in connection with a death, then The transfer is in connection with the death of the purchaser, which Rule 501(a)(4) excepts from the restriction. That limb is about the occasion, not the recipient: it does not require the transferee to be anyone in particular.; otherwise, if holding's in connection with a divorce, then The transfer is in connection with a divorce of the purchaser, which Rule 501(a)(4) excepts from the restriction. That limb is about the occasion, not the recipient.; otherwise, The transfer is in connection with a circumstance similar to a death or a divorce, which Rule 501(a)(4)'s closing words except from the restriction. Read as similar to a death or a divorce specifically — incapacity or a separation, for instance — and not as a general licence..

<!-- l4: decide:why the transfer stands or falls for -->

**40.** In relation to a ResaleFacts “holding”, why the transfer stands or falls for means if holding's selling back to the issuer, then You are transferring back to the issuer of the securities, which Rule 501(a)(1) excepts from the restriction.; otherwise, if holding's buyer is an accredited investor, then The transferee is an accredited investor, which Rule 501(a)(2) excepts from the restriction.; otherwise, if holding's part of a registered offering, then The transfer is part of an offering registered with the Commission, which Rule 501(a)(3) excepts from the restriction.; otherwise, if (a)(4) — a family or trust transferee, or a death-or-divorce circumstance with transfer from with holding, then which part of Rule 501(a)(4) applies to with holding; otherwise, if not transfer is within the one-year restricted period with transfer from with holding, then concatenate The one-year restricted period has run: the securities were issued , transfer from with holding's days since the securities were issued as string,  days ago, and Rule 501(a) restricts transfer for one year from issuance — here, until , the first anniversary of the issuance with transfer from with holding as string and .; otherwise, concatenate The securities were issued , transfer from with holding's days since the securities were issued as string,  days ago. Rule 501(a) restricts transfer for one year from issuance — here, until , the first anniversary of the issuance with transfer from with holding as string and  — and none of the four exceptions in Rule 501(a)(1)-(4) applies..

<!-- l4: decide:resale caveats -->

**41.** Resale caveats means list of The one-year period is measured to the calendar anniversary of the issuance date, so a period spanning 29 February is 366 days and any other is 365. The anniversary itself is read as OUTSIDE the period. Rule 501(a) says only 'the one-year period beginning when the securities were issued' and does not say whether the first anniversary falls inside it. That ambiguity is in the rule, not in this model, and no encoding can settle it., 'Accredited investor' takes its meaning from Rule 501 of Regulation D. This check takes your answer at face value. and Rule 501 restricts transfer by a purchaser in the crowdfunding transaction. It does not, by itself, tell you what a subsequent holder may do..

